{
  "dom_id": "step-t9523",
  "name": "Orientation-Independence",
  "problem": "Consider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F$ below. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14224093/assets/e679efaa2612d45bbfe8b918de7148853b64eaf530af34a525ef1735e0946b09.png)\n\nBy drawing an outward-pointing unit normal vector, it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14224093/assets/fbcd6fd0fadcc955212a145de9edb4654ddb92ddebb8ba31eb775cff676efeeb.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is positive, and we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s > 0.\n$$\n\nWhen measuring flux through a closed curve, the curve's orientation does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same in either case. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14224093/assets/a3d398d382e6bc94ddde77e650d50a6b5ec050083f89f64104f6f2fd172a9239.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is also positive, and we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s > 0.\n$$",
